+\paragraph{Local quota and one opening anchor.}
+Let $K_{\rm loc}\geq1$ be a configured local proposal quota, independent of
+the cross-region application quota $K$. A view $v$ opens at a physical block
+$G_v=B_{k_v^0}$. Its fresh positions are $k_v^0+1,\ldots,k_v^0+K_{\rm loc}$;
+the last is its local terminal block. APP and DRAIN each consume one local
+proposal position, while DRAIN still consumes no application quota.
+Re-certification of $G_v$ consumes no fresh position. Thus an ordinary
+application-only view produces $K_{\rm loc}$ batches. A completed outer
+session stops production and watchdogs even if this local quota is unfinished.
+
+The opening identifier is
+$\eta_v=\Hash(\Tag{START},s,v,k_v^0,\Hash(G_v),K_{\rm loc})$.
+The leader binds it in its first PROPOSE or NEWVIEW and every later proposal;
+VOTE signatures and QCs bind it as part of their signing context. A correct
+member votes under at most one opening identifier per view and at most once
+per $(s,v,k)$, including across entry modes. Two different openings signed
+by the view's leader are equivocation even when their anchors are comparable.
+The identifier uses block identity, not a certificate's signature encoding.
+There is no separate START message or signature exchange.
+
+A \emph{terminal QC} $N_v=Q_v(B_{k_v^0+K_{\rm loc}})$ includes evidence for
+this valid opening and its complete bounded ancestry. An inherited terminal
+block re-certified as another view's root is not that view's terminal QC.
+Each new valid QC is retained and forwarded once per certified block and
+view, independently of either leader.
+
+\paragraph{Entry and timer state.}
+Each member persists its largest entered view, its fixed entry locks and
+accepted openings, and a separate $\mathit{quit}_S[v]$ flag. A terminal QC
+$N_v$ or a valid quit proof for $v$ authenticates entry toward $v+1$.
+Future-view evidence must include or resolve its authenticated entry history;
+an unsupported view number does not activate a future leader. Normal
+advancement stops admitting fresh old-view proposals but is not a quit:
+it preserves old commit timers and continues to relay old certificates and
+conflict evidence. Only an authenticated quit of the timer's own view cancels
+that timer. Late evidence never decreases the current view.
 
