+\subsection{Targeted blame and safe inheritance}
+A local view ends normally through its terminal QC or early through a valid
+quit proof. The latter preserves the synchronous STATUS/NEWVIEW mechanism
+of Sync HotStuff. BLAME names the failed \emph{view}: since $\ell_i(v)$ is
+fixed, different observations of the ``current leader'' cannot silently
+change the target. Missing-progress complaints about one view contribute to its BLAME
+certificate; signatures naming different views may not be mixed.
+
+\paragraph{Proposal deadlines and certificate completion.}
+\label{sec:local-deadlines}
+Let $c_S(v)$ count distinct fresh view-$v$ proposals that triggered $S$'s
+votes. Re-certification and duplicates do not count. Let $D_S(v)$ be the
+first-fresh-proposal deadline, set once on entry and adjusted only by the
+single predecessor-quit rule below:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item On initial or recovery entry at local time $e_S(v)$, set
+$D_S(v)=e_S(v)+6\Delta_R$.
+\item On normal entry through $N_{v-1}$ verified at time $\tau_S$, if $S$
+previously voted for that terminal proposal at time $\theta_S$ while serving
+$v-1$, no predecessor quit is known, and $\tau_S\leq\theta_S+2\Delta_R$,
+set $D_S(v)=\theta_S+3\Delta_R$. Otherwise set
+$D_S(v)=\tau_S+2\Delta_R$ using immediate complete-object QC forwarding.
+\item If a quit proof for $v-1$ is first processed at $u_S$ before any fresh
+view-$v$ proposal has triggered a vote, allow the recovery path to finish:
+set $D_S(v)\gets\max\{D_S(v),u_S+7\Delta_R\}$. This covers one delay for
+quit dissemination, one for the remaining exit wait, two for NEWVIEW's
+collection window, and three for re-certification and first-proposal delivery.
+Apply this adjustment at most once; duplicate or older evidence never
+restarts a watchdog. If a first fresh proposal has already been accepted,
+the old proof does not restart this view's production schedule.
+\end{itemize}
+For $1\leq p\leq K_{\rm loc}$, the corresponding proposal deadline is
+$D_S(v)+2(p-1)\Delta_R$. There is no independent terminal-QC timeout.
+Once a valid proposal can trigger a correct member's vote, forwarding makes
+it available to correct peers within one delay; their multicast votes form
+its QC within another delay on the uninterrupted, ready path. Conflicting
+leader messages or a quit can instead interrupt that path and are handled
+by V1--V2. A replica must not infer that the next leader is lazy merely from
+its own temporarily incomplete vote set. The normal successor watchdog is
+armed only upon verification of the terminal QC.
+
+The proposal-based normal bound is
+\[
+ \begin{array}{c|l}
+ \theta_S & \text{validate terminal proposal, forward and vote}\\
+ \theta_S+\Delta_R & \text{correct members receive it and vote}\\
+ \theta_S+2\Delta_R & \text{correct votes form its QC, including at the successor}\\
+ \theta_S+3\Delta_R & \text{honest successor's first child reaches }S.
+ \end{array}
+\]
+The middle steps presume no conflict/quit interrupts the context. If a
+predecessor quit is learned, use the recovery allowance rather than accusing
+an honest successor that is still completing entry. No fixed wait is imposed
+on the normal proposer; these are upper bounds for detecting missing work.
+
